<header><p class="st-kicker">colony · West Africa</p><h1 class="st-title">British West Africa</h1><p class="st-meta">1821 CE – 1888 CE · capital Freetown</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The administrative grouping that placed Britain's scattered West African posts under one governor at Freetown for much of the 19th century.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>In 1821, after the dissolution of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/company-of-merchants-trading-to-africa">Company of Merchants</a>, <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/united-kingdom">Britain</a> placed its forts on the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/gold-coast-colony">Gold Coast</a> and its post on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/the-gambia">Gambia</a> under the governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sierra-leone">Sierra Leone</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-maccarthy">Charles MacCarthy</a>, as the British West African Settlements. The arrangement was meant to save money and to coordinate the campaign against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atlantic-slave-trade">slave trade</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The grouping was never stable. The <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/gold-coast-colony">Gold Coast</a> was returned to merchant rule in 1828. In 1843 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/the-gambia">Gambia</a> became a separate colony and the crown resumed control of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gold-coast">Gold Coast</a> forts, which received their own governor in 1850. In 1866 <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/united-kingdom">Britain</a> revived the scheme as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-africa">West Africa</a> Settlements, with a governor-in-chief at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/freetown">Freetown</a> over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sierra-leone">Sierra Leone</a>, the Gambia, the Gold Coast and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-lagos">Lagos</a>, each with an administrator. Distances, disease and poor communications made central control difficult.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/gold-coast-colony">Gold Coast</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-lagos">Lagos</a> were separated in 1874 as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gold-coast">Gold Coast</a> Colony, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/the-gambia">Gambia</a> regained its own governor in 1888, ending the arrangement. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sierra-leone">Sierra Leone</a> continued as the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/sierra-leone-colony-and-protectorate">Sierra Leone Colony and Protectorate</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/british-west-africa.jpg" alt="British West Africa" width="320" height="160"><figcaption>Image: Thommy · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_the_British_West_Africa_Settlements_(1870%E2%80%931888).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1821 CE – 1888 CE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>colony</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>colonial</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Freetown</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Charles MacCarthy, John Pope Hennessy</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Christianity, Islam, traditional religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>English</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/sierra-leone">Sierra Leone</a>, <a href="/countries/gambia">Gambia</a>, <a href="/countries/ghana">Ghana</a>, <a href="/countries/nigeria">Nigeria</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>British West African Settlements, West Africa Settlements</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1854&amp;focus=british-west-africa" aria-label="British West Africa on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4314 1861 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where British West Africa lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M4634 2117L4995 2226M4634 2117L5094 2192M4634 2117L4997 651M4634 2117L4546 1933M4634 2117L4634 2117"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M4995 2226h0M5094 2192h0M4997 651h0M4546 1933h0M4634 2117h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4634 2117h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4634 2117h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Freetown. <a href="/atlas?year=1854&amp;focus=british-west-africa">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

